{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.WenzlSchurElement"],"declarations":26,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.jonesMarkov_normalisation_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.jonesMarkov_normalisation_two","kind":"theorem","line":521,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L521-L535","doc":"**M1 normalisation at rank `2`.** Both partitions of `2` carry `f = 1`, and\n`y₍₂₎ + y₍₁,₁₎ = 1` follows from `telescope_two` divided by `Z₂`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.jonesMarkov_normalisation_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.jonesMarkov_normalisation_one","kind":"theorem","line":508,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L508-L519","doc":"**M1 normalisation at rank `1`.** The single partition `(1)` has `f = 1` and\n`y₍₁₎ = 1`, since `s₍₁₎ = Z₁`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.markov_peel_two_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.markov_peel_two_one","kind":"theorem","line":497,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L497-L504","doc":"**Concrete M3 peel at the load-bearing `(2,1)` shape** — the rank-2 → rank-3\ntransition. The corner-sum over the addable boxes of `(2,1)` equals `z` times its\nown Schur element, `z` the Markov parameter. Derived from the proved\n`pieriStep_two_one`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieriStep_iff_markov","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieriStep_iff_markov","kind":"theorem","line":489,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L489-L495","doc":"`PieriStep` **is** the statement that the rank-raising corner-sum multiplies\nthe Schur element by the Markov parameter `z`; naming `z` makes the M3 reading\nexplicit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescopeRHS_markov_recursion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescopeRHS_markov_recursion","kind":"theorem","line":482,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L482-L487","doc":"The same recursion on `telescopeRHS` (defeq to the Wenzl normalisation), the\nform the telescope induction step consumes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.wenzlNormalisation_markov_recursion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.wenzlNormalisation_markov_recursion","kind":"theorem","line":471,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L471-L480","doc":"**M3 peel at the normalisation level.** The Wenzl normalisation satisfies the\none-step Markov recursion `Z_{n+1} = z · Z_n` with `z` the Markov parameter. This\nis the trace-side content of the Markov property `tr(x·σ_n) = z·tr(x)`: raising\nthe rank by one multiplies the normalisation by exactly `z`, which is what the\nPieri corner-sum realises box-by-box. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.markovParameter","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.markovParameter","kind":"def","line":466,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L466-L469","doc":"The **Markov parameter** `z = (1 − X)/(1 − Q)` of the Jones–Markov trace. It\nis the single-box rank-raising factor: the Pieri corner-sum (`PieriStep`) equals\n`z · s_λ`, and the Wenzl normalisation obeys `Z_{n+1} = z · Z_n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.jonesMarkov_normalisation_of_telescope","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.jonesMarkov_normalisation_of_telescope","kind":"theorem","line":425,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L425-L462","doc":"**Markov-trace normalisation `tr_M(𝟙) = 1` at general rank `n`, conditional\non the Macdonald hook telescope.**\n\nGiven any list `parts` of `(multiplicity, partition)` pairs whose partitions all\nhave size `n`, IF the multiplicity-weighted Schur-element sum equals the Wenzl\nnormalisation `Z_n = ((1−X)/(1−Q))^n` (the general telescope, `htel` — the one\nidentity Mathlib's missing symmetric-function infrastructure prevents proving\noutright, see the file header), THEN the same multiplicities weight the concrete\nJones–Markov coefficients `y_λ = jonesMarkovCoeff` to exactly one:\n\n  `Σ_p  mult_p · s_{λ_p}  =  Z_n`   ⟹   `Σ_p  mult_p · y_{λ_p}  =  1`.\n\nThis is the honest conditional M1: it is the `jonesMarkov_normalisation_three`\nargument (divide the telescope by the common `Z_n`) lifted off the three fixed\npartitions of `3` to an arbitrary rank and partition list. It carries the open\ngeneral telescope as an explicit hypothesis rather than assuming it — instantiate\n`htel` with a proved telescope (as `telescope_three` does at `n = 3`) and the\nnormalisation follows unconditionally. Nonzero denominator needs only `Q ≠ 1`,\n`X ≠ 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.jonesMarkov_normalisation_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.jonesMarkov_normalisation_three","kind":"theorem","line":392,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L392-L421","doc":"**Markov-trace normalisation `tr_M(1) = 1` at rank `3`, for the concrete\nJones–Markov weights.** The closed-form weights `y_λ = jonesMarkovCoeff` sum to\none over the three partitions of `3`, each weighted by its SYT count\n`f_λ = (1, 2, 1)`:\n\n  `f₍₃₎·y₍₃₎ + f₍₂,₁₎·y₍₂,₁₎ + f₍₁³₎·y₍₁³₎ = 1`.\n\nThis is not assumed — it is **derived** from the proved `telescope_three`\n(`Σ_λ f_λ·s_λ = ((1−X)/(1−Q))³`) divided by the common Wenzl normalisation\n`Z₃ = ((1−X)/(1−Q))³`, which is nonzero for `Q ≠ 1`, `X ≠ 1`. Rank `3` is the\nfirst at which the standard channel `(2,1)` carries multiplicity `f = 2`, so the\nidentity genuinely exercises all three Wedderburn blocks — it is the M1 axiom\n`tr_M(𝟙) = 1` of the Markov trace, instantiated at the nucleon rank `H₃` on the\nreal weights rather than on an abstract trace functional. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieriStep_two_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieriStep_two_one","kind":"theorem","line":376,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L376-L388","doc":"`pieri_two_one` **is** (P) at `λ = (2,1)` — the enumeration produces exactly\nthe three corners that theorem sums, in the same order.\n\nThis is the load-bearing one: `(2,1)` is where the enumeration has to get the\nweak-decrease condition right (row 1 is strictly shorter than row 0, so it is\naddable) *and* remember the new bottom row. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieriStep_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieriStep_one","kind":"theorem","line":368,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L368-L374","doc":"`pieri_one` **is** (P) at `λ = (1)` — the enumeration produces exactly the\ntwo corners that theorem sums. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.PieriStep","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.PieriStep","kind":"def","line":363,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L363-L366","doc":"**(P) at one shape**: the corner sum equals `((1 − X)/(1 − Q))` times the\nshape's own Schur element. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieriSum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieriSum","kind":"def","line":359,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L359-L361","doc":"The left side of (P): the Schur elements of `λ`'s addable corners, summed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieri_two_one_summands_at_two_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieri_two_one_summands_at_two_three","kind":"theorem","line":333,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L333-L344","doc":"**The three corners contribute genuinely different amounts**, and one is\nnegative: at `Q = 2`, `X = 3` they are `22/9`, `40/63` and `−2/9`, summing to\n`20/7`. Recorded because a sum identity whose summands happened to be equal —\nor all of one sign — would be a much weaker check than it looks. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieri_two_one_at_two_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieri_two_one_at_two_three","kind":"theorem","line":321,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L321-L331","doc":"**(P) at `λ = (2,1)` is sharp at `Q = 2`, `X = 3`**: both sides are `20/7`,\nso the identity is not satisfied vacuously by both sides vanishing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieri_two_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieri_two_one","kind":"theorem","line":292,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L292-L319","doc":"**(P) at `λ = (2,1)`**: `s₍₃,₁₎ + s₍₂,₂₎ + s₍₂,₁,₁₎ = ((1 − X)/(1 − Q)) · s₍₂,₁₎`.\n\nThree corners, one of each kind — a row with something below it, a row with\nsomething above it in the changed column, and a new bottom row. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_two_via_pieri","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_two_via_pieri","kind":"theorem","line":260,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L260-L271","doc":"**The induction step, executed once.** `n = 2` derived from `n = 1` through\n`pieri_one` — not re-proved from scratch.\n\nThis is the point of the reduction: `telescope_two` above is proved directly,\nwhereas this obtains the same statement from the previous rung plus (P). If the\ngeneral induction is ever formalised, this is the shape it will take. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieri_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.pieri_one","kind":"theorem","line":241,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L241-L258","doc":"**(P) at `μ = (1)`**: `s₍₂₎ + s₍₁,₁₎ = ((1 − X)/(1 − Q)) · s₍₁₎`.\n\nThe two addable corners of a single box are `(2)` and `(1,1)`. Note this holds\nwith `X` free — it is not special to `X = Q^r`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_three_needs_multiplicity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_three_needs_multiplicity","kind":"theorem","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L183-L206","doc":"**The multiplicity is load-bearing.** Replacing `f₍₂,₁₎ = 2` by `1` breaks\nthe identity at `Q = 2`, `X = 3`. Stated so that \"the sum of Schur elements\"\ncannot be mistaken for the telescope: the *weighting* is part of the claim.\n\nAt `Q = 2, X = 3` the three Schur elements are `110/21`, `10/7`, `−2/21`. With\nmultiplicities `(1, 2, 1)` they sum to exactly `8`; unweighted they sum to\n`46/7`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_three_at_two_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_three_at_two_three","kind":"theorem","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L175-L181","doc":"**`n = 3` is sharp at `Q = 2`, `X = 3`**: the weighted sum is exactly `8`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_three","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L149-L173","doc":"**`n = 3`.** `s₍₃₎ + 2·s₍₂,₁₎ + s₍₁,₁,₁₎ = ((1−X)/(1−Q))³`.\n\nThe `2` is the number of standard Young tableaux of shape `(2,1)`. This is the\nfirst telescope case in which `f_λ ≠ 1` for some `λ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.old_contentSum_prefactor_breaks_telescope","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.old_contentSum_prefactor_breaks_telescope","kind":"theorem","line":115,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L115-L133","doc":"**With the OLD `contentSum` prefactor the identity FAILS**, at the same\npoint: the sum is `41/6`, not `4`.\n\nThis is the theorem that makes the bug fix a correction rather than a\npreference. If someone reverts `schurElement`'s prefactor to `contentSum`, this\nstatement — and `telescope_two` — become unprovable together. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_two_at_two_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_two_at_two_three","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L106-L113","doc":"**The corrected telescope is sharp at a point**: `Q = 2`, `X = 3` gives\nexactly `4 = ((1−3)/(1−2))²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_two","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L72-L91","doc":"**`n = 2`, the first case with content.** Both partitions of `2` have\n`f_λ = 1`, so the telescope reads `s₍₂₎ + s₍₁,₁₎ = ((1 − X)/(1 − Q))²`.\n\nThe hypotheses are exactly what the denominators and the `Q⁻¹` in the\n`(1,0)`-cell content require: `Q ≠ 0` for the negative power, and `Q ≠ 1`,\n`Q ≠ −1` for `1 − Q` and `1 − Q²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescope_one","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L64-L70","doc":"**`n = 1`.** The single partition `(1)` has `f = 1` and\n`s₍₁₎ = (1 − X)/(1 − Q)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescopeRHS","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MacdonaldTelescope.telescopeRHS","kind":"def","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MacdonaldTelescope.lean#L61-L62","doc":"The right-hand side of the telescope, `((1 − X)/(1 − Q))^n`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}